              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The question of whether Crimea belongs to Russia depends on the distinction between international law (*de jure* status) and actual, physical control (*de facto* status).\n\n### 1. Under International Law (*De Jure*): It is Ukraine\nThe vast majority of the international community recognizes Crimea as a sovereign part of **Ukraine**. \n* **The United Nations:** Following Russia's annexation of the peninsula in 2014, the United Nations General Assembly passed Resolution 68/262. This resolution, supported by a large majority of member states, affirmed the territorial integrity of Ukraine and declared the Russian-organized referendum in Crimea invalid.\n* **Global Stance:** The United States, the European Union, NATO, and almost all other nations continue to recognize Crimea as Ukrainian territory temporarily occupied by Russia. They view Russia\u2019s 2014 annexation as a violation of international law and a breach of the 1994 Budapest Memorandum, in which Russia had committed to respecting Ukraine\u2019s existing borders.\n\n### 2. In Practical Reality (*De Facto*): It is Administered by Russia\nIn practice, Crimea is currently controlled and administered by **Russia**.\n* **Control:** Since March 2014, Russian military and administrative authorities have exercised complete control over the peninsula. \n* **Integration:** Russia treats Crimea as a formal part of the Russian Federation, specifically dividing it into two federal subjects: the Republic of Crimea and the federal city of Sevastopol. Russian laws, currency (the ruble), taxes, school curricula, and time zones are fully operational there. \n\n### The Opposing Perspectives\n\n* **Ukraine's Position:** Ukraine considers Crimea to be its sovereign territory under temporary foreign occupation. Kyiv rejects any Russian claim to the peninsula, maintains that the 2014 referendum was held at gunpoint without international observers, and has vowed to restore its control over all Ukrainian territories.\n* **Russia's Position:** Russia considers Crimea to be an official and non-negotiable part of the Russian Federation. Moscow argues that the peninsula was historically Russian (having been transferred from the Russian SFSR to the Ukrainian SSR in 1954 under Soviet leader Nikita Khrushchev) and claims that the 2014 referendum represented a legitimate exercise of self-determination by the local population. \n\n**Summary:** \nWhile **Russia** exercises physical, administrative, and military control over Crimea (*de facto*), under international law and the official consensus of the United Nations, Crimea is recognized as part of **Ukraine** (*de jure*).",
